Q：反直觉的地理知识有哪些？
@Osamu：这 TM 是韩国


还有很多一样的地名.....
Q：你的学校发生过最扯的事是什么?
@一只萝 bro ：一帮小混混收保护费，有个乖宝宝交了，然后当他被堵在墙角的时候，他们开着鬼火来救他了。
Q：虾为啥要长得这么适合被吃？
@marg ：你吃过鸡腿吗？为了让你吃着方便，甚至鸡都长了一个把儿让你拿着方便！
Q：降水线真的北移了么？
@无为 ：前些天去库布其沙漠景区，工作人员在打除草剂除草。
Q：你见过最惊为天人的发型是什么样子？
@纯爱大祭司 K ：这哥们秃了，所以就给自己纹了点头发茬

Sheer joy of performing radiates from Big Jack Johnson and family members in Clarksdale, Mississippi. His guitar wails with the amplified notes of Chicago-style blues. Rooted in the Mississippi Delta, the blues sprang from spiritual music and field hollers, spinning a cultural thread that followed the Great Black Migration from South to North. &nbsp;
This story originally published in the April 1999 issue of National Geographic magazine. See more digitized stories from our archives here.
Samuel Reuben Kendrick, my great-grandfather, was born a slave in Alabama. In 1888 he founded a farming community called New Africa on 160 acres he bought from the railroad near Duncan, Mississippi. Among the tribulations he faced—floods, boll weevil infestations, bank loans due—one incident…

